================================================================================
 /etc/crontab
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW & PURPOSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The /etc/crontab file is the system-wide configuration file used by the cron 
daemon to schedule background, automated tasks. Unlike user-specific crontabs 
(managed via 'crontab -e'), /etc/crontab requires a username field before the 
command specification. This allows the system administrator to execute tasks as 
any valid user on the system (e.g., root, www-data, mysql).


2. STANDARD CONFIGURATION FILE TEMPLATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /etc/crontab: system-wide crontab
# Unlike any other crontab, you must define the user to run commands as.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin

# Example of job definition:
# .---------------- minute (0 - 59)
# |  .------------- hour (0 - 23)
# |  |  .---------- day of month (1 - 31)
# |  |  |  .------- month (1 - 12) OR jan, feb, mar, apr ...
# |  |  |  |  .---- day of week (0 - 6) (Sunday=0 or 7) OR sun, mon, tue, wed, thu, fri, sat
# |  |  |  |  |
# *  *  *  *  * user-name command-to-be-executed

 17  *  *  *  * root   cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.hourly
 25  6  *  *  * root   test -x /usr/sbin/anacron || ( cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.daily )
 47  6  *  *  7 root   test -x /usr/sbin/anacron || ( cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.weekly )
 52  6  1  *  * root   test -x /usr/sbin/anacron || ( cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.monthly )


3. SYNTAX BREAKDOWN & STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Environment Variables:
- SHELL: Defines the command interpreter shell (commonly /bin/sh or /bin/bash).
- PATH: Defines the directories searched for executables. System crontabs have a 
  strict, restricted path; always use absolute paths for custom scripts.

The Five Time & Date Fields:
1. Minute (0-59): The exact minute of the hour the task runs.
2. Hour (0-23): The hour in 24-hour format.
3. Day of Month (1-31): The calendar day.
4. Month (1-12 or names like jan, feb): The month of execution.
5. Day of Week (0-7 where both 0 and 7 are Sunday, or names like mon, tue).

Special Characters:
- '*' (Asterisk): Means "every" possible value for that field.
- ',' (Comma): Specifies a list of multiple values (e.g., 1,15,30).
- '-' (Hyphen): Defines a continuous range of values (e.g., 9-17).
- '/' (Forward Slash): Specifies step values or increments (e.g., */10).


4. DETAILED EXPLANATION OF SYSTEM MAINTENANCE JOBS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A) Hourly Maintenance Job:
   17 * * * * root   cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.hourly
   - Timing: Runs at the 17th minute of every hour.
   - User: Executed with administrative privileges as root.
   - Action: Changes working directory to root and executes all scripts 
     stored inside the /etc/cron.hourly/ directory.

B) Daily Maintenance Job:
   25 6 * * * root   test -x /usr/sbin/anacron || ( cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.daily )
   - Timing: Runs every day at 6:25 AM.
   - User: Executed as root.
   - Action: Checks if anacron is installed and executable. If anacron is 
     present, it skips the cron daily execution (preventing duplicate tasks if 
     the system was offline). Otherwise, it executes all scripts in /etc/cron.daily/.

C) Weekly Maintenance Job:
   47 6 * * 7 root   test -x /usr/sbin/anacron || ( cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.weekly )
   - Timing: Runs every week on Sunday at 6:47 AM (7 = Sunday).
   - User: Executed as root.
   - Action: Checks for anacron; if unavailable, runs all scripts inside 
     /etc/cron.weekly/.

D) Monthly Maintenance Job:
   52 6 1 * * root   test -x /usr/sbin/anacron || ( cd / && run-parts --report /etc/cron.monthly )
   - Timing: Runs on the 1st day of every month at 6:52 AM.
   - User: Executed as root.
   - Action: Checks for anacron; if unavailable, runs all scripts inside 
     /etc/cron.monthly/.


5. BEST PRACTICES FOR SYSTEM ADMINISTRATORS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Always Use Absolute Paths: Cron runs with a barebones environment. Specify full 
   paths for binaries and scripts (e.g., /usr/bin/python3 /opt/scripts/main.py).
2. Capture Output Logs: Explicitly redirect stdout and stderr to avoid silent failures 
   or mail spool bloating:
   0 2 * * * root /usr/local/bin/backup.sh >> /var/log/sys_backup.log 2>&1
3. Directory-Based Scheduling: Instead of bloating /etc/crontab, drop script symlinks 
   directly into /etc/cron.hourly/, /etc/cron.daily/, etc.
